okay today I'm going to talk to you  about biotic and abiotic factors what  these are are different factors that can  affect the population of a particular  species within a habitat so to begin  with I'm going to talk to you about  biotic factors now the prefixed bio  means living so biotic factors are  living things that can change in alter  the population of other living things so  we're going to have a look at two  different aspects of this so first of  all we're going to look at this thing  called competition now a nice  illustration of this is with squirrels  there's two types of squirrel that still  live them in the UK the red squirrel and  the grey squirrel now they used to be  many many more red squirrels living  across the UK but in about the 1800s the  grey squirrels were introduced to the UK  now they were both competing for the  same resources for the same food for  example now the grey squirrels being  somewhat stronger and bigger out  competed the red squirrels so the  introduction of this these grey  squirrels a biotic factor because the  grey squirrels are living it affected  the population of the red squirrels so  competition is a really good example of  an alteration in a biotic factor the  changing of a biotic factor another key  point is predators another biotic factor  that could be introduced so imagine  let's say we have both of these  squirrels coexist in if we were to  introduce a predator such as this Fox  here again it's a biotic factor because  it's a living thing it would alter the  numbers of these squirrels because the  frogs could prey on these squirrels so  the number of squirrels the population  could decrease so again we're looking at  a biotic factor the other type of  factors that we're going to look at are  abiotic factors now these are the  nonliving things that could alter the  population of an organism within an eco  so there's four things that we're going  to look at number one is temperature so  remember temperature is abiotic because  temperature is obviously nonliving so  I'm just going to draw a quick picture  of a thermometer so essentially  different animals and plants thrive best  at different temperatures some would  thrive best in higher temperatures some  thrive fest in lower temperatures so the  temperature can have an effect on the  distribution of organisms within an  ecosystem the next fire factor abiotic  again because it's nonliving is water so  the the amount of water can massively  influence the distribution of organisms  for example daisies grow best in soils  that are slightly damp so if there's if  there's too much water or it's too dry  will have no daisies whatsoever and  that's just one illustration of how  water affects the distribution of  organisms let's say there's a drought if  there's very little water at all  there'll be very few plants growing  whatsoever because plants require water  in order to photosynthesize so those are  to a buttock factors the next one is  light intensity so how much light there  is available within an ecosystem so this  can also massively influence the  distribution of organisms for example  certain certain things like fungi and  mosses grow best in dark conditions so  whereas things like grasses may be  better in lighter conditions so the  light intensity again abiotic is  nonliving massively affects the  population of different organisms and  finally the last one we're going to look  at is pollutants and here's just a  little power plant I'll draw here  okay so pollutants can also affect the  population of diff  species for example sulfur dioxide so2  can mean that certain things called  lichens it affects the growth of certain  organisms called lichens which means  they can't grow you can actually learn  more about this in the video about  indicator species but again if we have  certain pollutants it might cause a  decrease in certain organisms or in the  case of eutrophication again in another  video we can see that pollutants can  actually increase the growth or coverage  the growth of plants like algae so just  to summarize there's two types of  factors biotic and abiotic biotic a  living so if animals compete against  each other they can affect each other's  population if we introduce predators it  can again cause a decrease in the  population of the prey the for abiotic  factors you need to know a temperature  water light intensity and pollutants and  all four of these can also influence the  distribution and population of organisms  within an ecosystem thank you
 

